  </div>

  <main class="deck" id="deck">

    <!-- ==================================================
         SLIDE 1 — TITLE
    ================================================== -->

    <section class="slide" data-title="Title">
      <div class="container center">

        <div class="eyebrow">
          PLS 210 · Presentation #1 · Fall 2026
        </div>

        <h1>
          Remembering the<br>
          <span class="accent">First Presidents</span>
        </h1>

        <p class="intro">
          Citizens’ Interpretations of Public Commemoration
          in Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan
        </p>

        <div class="chips">
          <span class="chip">Political memory</span>
          <span class="chip">Public commemoration</span>
          <span class="chip">Interpretivism</span>
        </div>

        <p class="small" style="margin-top: 34px;">
          Elmira Bobkova
        </p>

      </div>
    </section>


    <!-- ==================================================
         SLIDE 2 — OPENING PUZZLE
    ================================================== -->

    <section class="slide" data-title="The puzzle">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="eyebrow">
          The research puzzle
        </div>

        <h2>
          Public symbols are visible.<br>
          <span class="accent">Their meanings are not.</span>
        </h2>

        <p class="intro">
          Imagine walking past a monument or along a street
          named after a former president.
        </p>

        <div class="statement">
          Does that symbol represent national history,
          a personal memory, an official message —
          or something you barely notice?
        </div>

        <p class="description" style="margin-top: 24px;">
          The existence of a commemorative symbol does not
          tell us how citizens understand it.
        </p>

      </div>
    </section>


    <!-- ==================================================
         SLIDE 3 — KAZAKHSTAN PHOTOS 1–3
    ================================================== -->

    <section class="slide" data-title="Kazakhstan: public space">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="eyebrow">
          Case 01 · Kazakhstan
        </div>

        <h2>
          Commemoration in
          <span class="accent">public space</span>
        </h2>

        <p class="description">
          Presidential commemoration can appear in monuments
          and the names of places people encounter in everyday life.
        </p>

        <div class="gallery">

          <!-- PICTURE 1 -->

          <figure class="photo">
